{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":277258,"num_turns":1,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"3ec01e03-0cb6-4547-ae75-610df58ddb67","total_cost_usd":1.350488,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":69350,"cache_read_input_tokens":20278,"output_tokens":23830,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":69350,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":23830,"cache_read_input_tokens":20278,"cache_creation_input_tokens":69350,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":69350},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":50989,"outputTokens":20,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.051089,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty"},"claude-opus-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":23830,"cacheReadInputTokens":20278,"cacheCreationInputTokens":69350,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":1.299399,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\n  \"agent\": \"spec-auditor\",\n  \"verdict\": \"fail\",\n  \"summary\": \"Seven of twenty checks fail: the ship cannot reach any band, the cheapest descent is a single plunge at every band and load, the skim cooling multiplier the whole ablation economy is priced off is wrong by up to 0.55 against measurement, the full hold overshoots the doubling rule at 2.64x, and the stated canopy flies a full hold at 5.45 m/s rather than the claimed 4.60.\",\n  \"checks\": [\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"band_names_consistent\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.7\",\n      \"statement\": \"Every band name used in the catalog or the params appears in the baseline's bands, and no undefined band is referenced.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Baseline defines {suborbital, low, high}. Catalog debris[].band uses exactly those three (9 suborbital, 9 low, 10 high entries) and band_summary lists the same three. Params reference bands in ablation.heat_index {suborbital, low, high}, ablation.cost_curve {suborbital, low, high}, ablation.optimal_skims {suborbital, low, high} and economy.band_value_multiplier {suborbital, low, high}. No fourth name appears anywhere; Armstrong's satellite is filed under 'high' rather than inventing a band above it.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"band_summary_matches_debris\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.7\",\n      \"statement\": \"Each band_summary entry agrees with the debris array it summarises on piece_count, mean_mass_kg and fragile_fraction.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Suborbital: 9 pieces counted, claimed 9. Mass sum 430+45+85+130+35+700+380+70+1100 = 2975, /9 = 330.56 vs claimed 330.6. Spawn weight total 9+16+13+15+5+7+10+3+2 = 80, fragile weight 5+3 = 8, 8/80 = 0.100 vs claimed 0.1. Low: 9 pieces; sum 870+760+390+1150+340+155+210+420+2000 = 6295, /9 = 699.44 vs claimed 699.4; weights total 65, fragile 6+4+3 = 13, 13/65 = 0.200 vs claimed 0.2. High: 10 pieces; sum 1950+1600+2200+1150+700+920+1300+540+3200+4200 = 17760, /10 = 1776.0 vs claimed 1776; weights total 56, fragile 6+5+2+1 = 14, 14/56 = 0.250 vs claimed 0.25. All nine figures reproduce exactly.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"mean_mass_rises_by_band\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.7\",\n      \"statement\": \"Mean piece mass rises band over band, so valuable junk physically fights you.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"330.6 -> 699.4 -> 1776.0 kg, steps of 2.12x and 2.54x, 5.37x end to end. Restricted to hand-tetherable pieces and spawn-weighted the ordering holds too: suborbital 16730/78 = 214.5 kg, low 35220/63 = 559.0 kg, high (excluding the two crane-only pieces and the satellite) 1950*9+1600*8+2200*8+1150*9+700*6+920*6+1300*5+540*2 = 66370 over weight 53 = 1252.3 kg.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"fragile_share_rises_by_band\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.7\",\n      \"statement\": \"Fragile spawn share rises across the bands, roughly 1 in 10 suborbital to 1 in 4 high.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Spawn-weighted fragile share is 8/80 = 0.100 suborbital, 13/65 = 0.200 low, 14/56 = 0.250 high. That is exactly the 1-in-10 to 1-in-4 gradient GDD 2.3.7 asks for, monotonically increasing, with the low band landing halfway between.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"full_hold_doubles_mass\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.5\",\n      \"statement\": \"A full hold roughly doubles the rocket's mass at starting storage size.\",\n      \"result\": \"fail\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Measured cargo is 232.96 kg per slot, so 6 base slots = 1397.8 kg against dry_mass_kg 850: (850+1397.8)/850 = 2.64x, which is 32% past doubling and outside the ~25% tolerance. My own spawn-weighted arithmetic agrees: crushed mass per slot is 16730/102 = 164.0 kg suborbital and 35220/121 = 291.1 kg low, a slice mean near 227.6 kg, giving 6 x 227.6 = 1366 kg and 2.61x. The Balancer's balance_notes derive 1.88-2.16x from a 124.9 kg/slot figure, but that figure is the strict tier-1-compactor suborbital-only case, not what the simulator flew. The overshoot is the root of the parachute failures below: it is 649 kg of unbudgeted cargo mass under the same canopy.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"Raise dry_mass_kg from 850 toward roughly 1,300-1,400 kg so 6 slots x 233 kg/slot lands inside 1.75-2.25x, and re-derive the parachute area against the new full-hold mass.\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"skim_cost_curve_optimum_in_range\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"statement\": \"The skim cost curve's minimum is where optimal_skims says it is, the high band's optimum is 1 or 2, no band exceeds it, and the curve is what the stated model produces.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Model from balance_notes, with base 3.0, growth 1.8, coef 0.00045, exp 2.4, skim_peak 22 (skim term 0.00045*22^2.4 = 0.750). Cycle tolls: k=0 3.00, k=1 8.40, k=2 18.12, k=3 35.62. Recomputed points: suborbital k=0 = 3.00 + 0.00045*78^2.4 (=15.64) = 18.64 vs array 18.64; suborbital k=2 = 18.12 + 0.00045*52.26^2.4 (=5.98) + 1.50 = 25.60 vs 25.6. Low k=1 = 8.40 + 0.00045*85.1^2.4 (=19.28) + 0.75 = 28.43 vs 28.43; low k=3 = 35.62 + 0.00045*41.1^2.4 (=3.36) + 2.25 = 41.23 vs 41.23. High k=0 = 3.00 + 53.29 = 56.29 vs 56.29; high k=2 = 18.12 + 0.00045*87.1^2.4 (=20.38) + 1.50 = 40.00 vs 40.0. All eight recomputed points match to under 0.1%. Minima are at index 0 / 1 / 2 for suborbital / low / high, which equals optimal_skims 0/1/2 exactly; the high band's optimum of 2 is inside 1-2 and no band exceeds it. The curve is a faithful evaluation of its own coefficients — but see skim_multiplier_matches_measured: one of those coefficients is wrong, which is a separate failure and not this one.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"cheapest_descent_is_multi_pass\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"statement\": \"The cheapest descent is 2-4 committed passes, never a single plunge and never feathering.\",\n      \"result\": \"fail\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Judged only on flown pass counts. cheapest_pass_count is 1 in all nine band x load descents, and ablation_by_pass_count is monotonically increasing everywhere. Suborbital empty: 15.2 / 21.9 / 31.7 / 49.3 / 80.9 / 137.6 / 239.7 percent of plate at 1-7 passes. Low full hold: 39.9 / 54.1 / 65.6 / 83.6 / 114.9. High full hold: 37.4 / 59.1 / 71.9 / 90.6 / 123.4. The failure is a single plunge, not feathering. It is structural rather than mistuned: braking altitude climbs with pass count inside a 43,000 m atmosphere (suborbital empty 29,357 m at 1 pass to 37,776 m at 4; high full hold 23,098 m to 28,062 m), so extra passes brake in thinner air and peak heat barely moves (suborbital empty 70 -> 74, high full hold 108 -> 119). With peak heat essentially flat in pass count, the heat-dependent term has nothing to give back and the thermal-cycling toll has nothing to trade against, so no assignment of cycle_toll_base_pct, cycle_toll_growth, heat_cost_coefficient or heat_cost_exponent can move the minimum off 1.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"No value in params can satisfy this rule at this flight model — the finding is against GDD 2.3.1 itself, which needs either the skim framing substituted for the pass framing or the GDD 4.5 risk-1 fallback (flat per-pass ablation with a hard pass cap) adopted by a human.\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"skim_multiplier_matches_measured\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"statement\": \"The claimed skim_heat_multiplier matches the multiplier the simulator measured for the same quantity in the high band.\",\n      \"result\": \"fail\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Claimed [1.0, 0.851, 0.670, 0.411] against measured high-band [1.0, 0.985, 0.971, 0.958] at the fixed entry depth of 0 m. Index-by-index deltas 0.000 / 0.134 / 0.301 / 0.547 — three of the four exceed the 0.10 tolerance, the last by more than fivefold. Three skims were measured to remove 4.2% of peak heat (161.5 -> 155.0), not the 58.9% claimed. Low band measures [1.0, 0.975, 0.953, 0.932] and suborbital [1.0, 0.948, 0.891, 0.733], so the single array applied to all three bands is wrong for all three. Consequence, priced against the params' own toll: the first high-band skim now buys 2.4 heat points for a marginal cycle toll of 3.0*1.8 + 0.75 = 6.15 plate-percent, so the cost_curve above — arithmetically faithful though it is — collapses to a minimum at 0 skims in every band once the measured array is substituted, and optimal_skims 0/1/2 goes with it.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"Adopt the measured high-band array [1.0, 0.985, 0.971, 0.958] into skim_heat_multiplier and re-derive cost_curve and optimal_skims from it; the anti-feathering job then has to be done by cycle_toll_growth alone, or conceded.\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"skim_cooling_saturates_fatigue_bounds\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"statement\": \"skim_heat_multiplier starts at 1.0, is non-increasing and flattens by its last two entries, and cycle_toll_growth exceeds 1.\",\n      \"result\": \"fail\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Two legs pass and one fails. Starts at 1.0: yes. Non-increasing: yes, 1.0 > 0.851 > 0.670 > 0.411. cycle_toll_growth 1.8 > 1: yes, so the toll is geometric and does bound skim count. Saturation fails: successive drops are 0.149, 0.181, 0.259 — the curve is accelerating downward, steepest at the last step, which is the opposite of flattening. It claims a cooling that keeps compounding at the grazing limit, and the measured arrays show that cooling does not exist at all (high band's last two steps are 0.014 and 0.013, and at 21,500 m entry depth it weakens further to [1.0, 0.989, 0.979, 0.971]). The failure is in the multiplier's shape, not in the fatigue toll.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"Replace skim_heat_multiplier with a measured, saturating array whose last two steps are near-equal; leave cycle_toll_growth above 1 where it already is.\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"parachute_full_hold_under_soft_line\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.2\",\n      \"statement\": \"Parachute descent speed at full hold is under the 5 m/s soft-landing threshold and above about 3.5 m/s, so the Parachute upgrade is a real purchase.\",\n      \"result\": \"fail\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Measured full-hold terminal velocity is 5.45 m/s, over the soft_landing_ms line of 5, and soft_landing is false in all three full-hold descents (suborbital, low and high all report touchdown_ms 5.45). Recomputed: v = sqrt(2*2247.8*9.0/(1.5*1.8*504)) = sqrt(40460.4/1360.8) = sqrt(29.73) = 5.45 m/s. The claimed 4.60 comes from a 1,599 kg ship; the flown ship is 2,247.8 kg. The hard-landing line therefore falls between half hold (4.53 m/s, soft) and full hold rather than beyond full hold, so a base hold is already a hard landing before any greed.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"Raise parachute_area_m2 from 504 to roughly 710 m2 (504 * (5.45/4.6)^2 = 707) at the measured 2,247.8 kg, or cut the full-hold mass via dry_mass_kg per full_hold_doubles_mass and re-derive.\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"parachute_speed_matches_flight\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.2\",\n      \"statement\": \"The claimed descent speed is what the stated canopy actually flies.\",\n      \"result\": \"fail\",\n      \"evidence\": \"parachute.independent is true, so the area was stated (504 m2 at Cd 1.8) and the measurement is a real second opinion rather than a tautology — the rule was genuinely testable this run. It fails on magnitude: claimed_full_hold_ms 4.6 against measured_full_hold_ms 5.45, delta_ms 0.85. Tolerance is 5% of the claim = 0.23 m/s; the delta is 18.5% of the claim, 3.7x over. The disagreement is entirely in the mass term: 2*9.0*2247.8/1360.8 = 29.73 giving 5.45, against the notes' 2*9.0*1599/1360.8 = 21.15 giving 4.60. spread_across_bands_ms is 0, so this is a mass error and not a band-dependent one.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"Recompute descent_speed_full_hold_ms from the flown full-hold mass of 2,247.8 kg rather than the assumed 1,599 kg, and move parachute_area_m2 until the recomputed figure is the one stated.\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"tow_fee_clamps_linear_never_negative\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.3\",\n      \"statement\": \"The tow fee is zero inside the free radius, linear beyond it, clamps at exactly 50%, and can never go negative or exceed the cap.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"free_radius_m 1200, curve 'linear', max_fee_fraction 0.5. Half circumference against the baseline's R = 200,000 m is pi*200000 = 628,318 m, so fee_fraction = 0.5 * clamp((d - 1200)/(628318 - 1200), 0, 1). At d = 0 and d = 1200 the numerator is <= 0 and the clamp floors it at 0. At d = 314,759 m (midpoint) it is 0.5 * 0.5 = 0.250. At d = 628,318 m it is 0.5 * 1.0 = 0.500 exactly, and the clamp holds it there for any greater distance, so payout never falls below 50% and never rises above 100%. The clamp's lower bound is what forbids a negative fee, and the fee is levied on the haul only, so it cannot reach savings.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"lazy_run_breaks_even\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.6\",\n      \"statement\": \"Launch cost is at or below the value of three cheap suborbital pieces.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The cheapest piece in the game is a suborbital small: size_class_base_value.small 40 x band_value_multiplier.suborbital 1.0 = 40. Three of them = 120. launch_cost is 80, which is 2.00 cheap pieces and inside GDD 2.3.6's stated 2-3. Adding the replate bill for a clean empty suborbital descent at the measured cheapest ablation of 15.2% x replate_cost_per_pct 2 = 30.4 puts a lazy run's total cost at 110.4, still under the 120 that three cheap pieces pay.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"steady_tow_never_shears\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.4\",\n      \"statement\": \"Steady-state towing tension never reaches magnet_hold_force_n.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Astronaut acceleration is jetpack_thrust_n / (suit_mass_kg + total towed mass), so per-cable tension is 700*m_i/(250 + sum m) and the sum across all cables is at most 700 N — below magnet_hold_force_n 900 for any load at any tether count, by construction. Heaviest legal two-piece load with base_tether_slots 2 is the two heaviest hand-tetherable pieces in the catalog, cryo_propellant_sphere 2200 + comsat_bus_frame 1950 = 4150 kg: a = 700/4400 = 0.159 m/s2, tension on the heavy cable = 2200*0.159 = 350 N, 39% of hold force. The worst single case is one 2200 kg piece alone: 700*2200/2450 = 629 N, 70% of hold force, leaving 271 N of margin. No steady tow shears.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"staging_is_one_way\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"statement\": \"No parameter reintroduces thrust after staging.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The only thrust values in params are flight.thrust_n 26000, flight.rcs_thrust_n 1500 — both attached to the stage that is jettisoned — and eva.jetpack_thrust_n 700, which is the astronaut's pack and is exactly the get-out-and-push rescue GDD 2.3.1 sanctions (700 N against a 2,247.8 kg full-hold ship is 0.31 m/s2, a nudge and not an engine). There is no post_stage thrust field, no residual-fuel field, and no upgrade in the twelve that adds thrust to a staged configuration; the six upgradable effects are fuel_capacity_kg, thrust_n, base_slots, plate_capacity_pct, parachute_area_m2 and magnet_hold_force_n.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"fragile_never_crushable_premium_sufficient\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.4\",\n      \"statement\": \"Fragile pieces are never crushable at any compactor tier present, and the fragile premium makes one intact fragile piece worth several solid ones of the same class and band.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Crushability: cargo.compactor_tier is 1 and no compactor upgrade appears among the twelve purchases, so tier 1 (small only) is the only tier in the shipping slice; no parameter grants crushing by fragile flag, and the catalog carries fragile as a per-piece boolean independent of size_class, so no tier can reach it. Premium: fragile_value_premium 3 multiplies on top of class and band, so a cracked_solar_array (low, medium, fragile) is 90 x 2.4 x 3 = 648 against 216 for a solid low-band medium — exactly 3.0 solid pieces of the same class and band. High band: gallium_solar_panel 90 x 5.5 x 3 = 1485 against 495. 'Several' holds at 3x, and the premium is paid only if the piece survives fragile_multiplier 2 damage from heat and landing.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"oversized_rejects_hand_magnet\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.4\",\n      \"statement\": \"Oversized junk rejects the hand magnet.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"catalog.size_classes.oversized.hand_tetherable is false, against true for small and medium. Slot costs are 6 crushed / 12 uncrushed, more hold than the 6 base slots, consistent with crane-cable-only handling. The three oversized pieces (collapsed_launch_shroud 1100 kg, derelict_upper_stage_tank 2000 kg, high_gain_dish_assembly 3200 kg) plus armstrongs_satellite 4200 kg all inherit that flag; none is reachable with the hand magnet the slice actually ships.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"twelve_upgrade_purchases\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.6\",\n      \"statement\": \"Twelve purchases: six parts, two tiers each, tier 2 dearer than tier 1, each tier improving the stat it names.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"12 entries, 6 parts x 2 tiers: fuel_tank, thruster, storage, heat_shield, parachute, hand_magnet. Tier 2 > tier 1 on cost for every part: 1100>450, 1200>500, 1400>600, 1300>550, 950>400, 900>350. Every stat improves monotonically from its base: fuel_capacity_kg 900 -> 1200 -> 1600; thrust_n 26000 -> 34000 -> 44000; base_slots 6 -> 9 -> 12; plate_capacity_pct 100 -> 150 -> 220; parachute_area_m2 504 -> 760 -> 1050; magnet_hold_force_n 900 -> 1400 -> 2000. No part names a stat that does not exist in the params.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"shipping_slice_bands_reachable\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"4.1\",\n      \"statement\": \"Every band the shipping slice needs — suborbital and low — is reachable, and the catalog populates both with at least one fragile piece and at least two size classes.\",\n      \"result\": \"fail\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The catalog half holds: suborbital carries small, medium and oversized with two fragile pieces (cracked_antenna_whip, shattered_lens_cowl), and low carries small, medium and oversized with three (cracked_solar_array, star_tracker_head, parabolic_dish_section), so the player meets crushing and fragility inside the slice. Reachability fails outright. The simulator flew all three ascents and reached none: suborbital 65,000 m reached=false 'crashed on ascent' with fuel_remaining_kg 0 and fuel_margin_pct 0, low 115,000 m the same, high 215,000 m the same. The params' own budget is why it is tight even on paper — exhaust velocity 26000/11.7 = 2222 m/s, wet mass 850+900 = 1750 kg, ideal dv = 2222*ln(1750/850) = 1605 m/s against roughly 1450 m/s claimed to the suborbital band, a 10% margin, at a liftoff TWR of 26000/(1750*9.0) = 1.65 where gravity losses eat more than that. Neither band of the shipping slice can currently be flown, which makes every descent figure in this audit a measurement of a flight the player cannot start.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"Raise thrust_n (liftoff TWR 1.65 is where the gravity losses are coming from) and fuel_capacity_kg together until the suborbital ascent closes with real margin, rather than treating fuel_tank tier 1 as a required first purchase.\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"no_target_moved_to_pass\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.2\",\n      \"statement\": \"No rule was satisfied by moving a quantity the design document fixes in prose.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Every GDD-fixed number is at its stated value. cargo.base_slots 6 matches Section 2.2's 'Cargo reads 4 of 6' and the appendix's '6 cargo slots'. eva.base_tether_slots 2 matches the appendix's '2 tethers'. landing.soft_landing_ms 5 matches 2.3.2's under-5 m/s line, no_gear_multiplier 2 and fragile_multiplier 2 match its double-damage rules. tow_fee.max_fee_fraction 0.5 matches 2.3.3's cap. reentry.cargo_damage_interval_s 3 matches 2.3.1's 'one cargo slot every ~3 seconds' and heat_dissipation_s 5 its 5-second dissipation. eva.magnet_range_screens 0.125 matches 2.3.4's 1/8 screen. cargo.compactor_tier 1 matches the 4.1 slice scope. Nothing was widened to let an arrow hit; the failures above are genuine misses against unmoved targets. The one number I would have expected to move and did not is dry_mass_kg 850, which the Balancer states it pinned to make the doubling rule true — and by measurement it does not, so it did not buy a pass either.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"observations\": [\n    \"reentry.off_retrograde_penalty is 1, which is no penalty at all. GDD 2.3.1 says the shield protects the shield-facing side only and heat builds faster with the cosine of the off-retrograde angle. At 1.0 the player can tumble through the entry and take the same heat as a perfectly held attitude, which quietly removes the one piloting skill the shielded pass is supposed to test.\",\n    \"Difficulty falls with altitude rather than rising. Optimal-pass ablation is 15.2 / 13.9 / 13.0 percent empty and 44.3 / 39.9 / 37.4 full hold for suborbital / low / high, and peak heat runs the same way (117 / 111 / 108 full hold). The band-indexed heat_index of 78 / 100 / 130 encodes the intent but the flight model does the opposite, because a higher band enters shallower and brakes deeper where the air is thicker. Cargo is what actually carries the curve: a full hold costs about 2.9x an empty one in every band. GDD 2.3.7's climb is currently a mass curve wearing an altitude label.\",\n    \"Ablation is recorded well past 100% of a plate that cannot exceed 100% — low band empty reaches 7,819% at 13 passes and high band empty 147,561% at 18. Those rows are arithmetic, not flights; a plate that hits 100% has already burned through cargo and hull. Whatever consumes this table should stop at plate_capacity_pct rather than reporting five-figure percentages as if they were descents.\",\n    \"Skims cost 0.51-0.53 hours each and 44-45 m/s of commit dv on the high band. Against a flight budget that cannot presently close an ascent, the skim manoeuvre is priced in a currency the ship does not have — and at 2.26 simulated hours for a three-skim high-band descent, the on-rails time warp GDD 4.1 deferred is a hard requirement for it, not a convenience.\",\n    \"The unstaged coarse-braking pass is the one thing this config does that almost nothing else in the sweep space does: shallow unstaged passes peak at 69 and 76 against a capacity of 100, survivable in both slice bands, bought by an unstaged ballistic coefficient of 609.3 kg/m2. It is worth knowing before the revision that every plausible fix above — lowering dry mass for the doubling rule, enlarging the canopy, lightening the ship for the ascent — spends exactly that margin.\",\n    \"One dry_mass_kg cannot make the doubling rule true in more than one band. Six slots of suborbital junk is 749-984 kg, six slots of high-band junk is roughly 6,600 kg (cryo_propellant_sphere alone is 2,200 kg for 2 crushed slots, 2.6x the whole dry ship). The Balancer balanced against suborbital, which is the case GDD 2.3.5 specifies and the slice actually ships, but the high band will need its own answer when it arrives.\",\n    \"Armstrong's satellite at 4,200 kg is 4.9x dry_mass_kg 850, where GDD 2.3.7 requires it to roughly double a fully upgraded ship. The two claims reconcile only if the maxed ship lands near 2,100 kg dry, which nothing in the current upgrade list produces — no upgrade in the twelve changes ship mass at all. The win condition's headline stat is currently unbacked by any number in the params.\",\n    \"The economy is priced against a descent nobody can fly. Every payout figure assumes a hold that comes home, and the measured full hold does not land soft in any band. Until the parachute and mass findings resolve, the lazy-run break-even that passes above is the only economy claim in this audit resting on a flight the simulator actually completed.\"\n  ]\n}","ttft_ms":153650,"ttft_stream_ms":5332,"time_to_request_ms":215,"type":"result","duration_ms":274883,"uuid":"1cc99560-2134-49b0-91fa-c91c10bd7edc"}
